.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-SET-BUCKET-INTERNAL 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-set-bucket-internal \- hide a bucket from listings, or stop hiding it
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm set-bucket-internal
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I NAME
.RB [ \-\-internal | \-i
.IR true | false ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Marks a bucket as euclid's own plumbing, or stops doing so.
.PP
An internal bucket works in every way an ordinary one does \- objects
are written, read, copied, moved and deleted exactly the same \- but it
is left out of
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-buckets (1)
and the bucket count, so it does not clutter a listing for people who
have no reason to act on it.
.SH WHAT THIS IS FOR
The bucket applications are deployed from. Its contents are artifacts
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1)
puts there and replaces on a redeploy \- nobody edits them by hand, and
a new version arrives by redeploying the application rather than by
uploading into the bucket. Somebody browsing their own buckets has no
reason to see it, and every reason to be confused by it.
.PP
The same applies to any bucket that exists because a component needed
somewhere to put something, rather than because a person wanted a
bucket.
.SH HIDDEN, NOT PROTECTED
This changes what is listed, not what is allowed. Anyone who knows the
name can still read and write an internal bucket \- which is exactly
what the component that created it does, and why hiding it cannot also
mean locking it.
.PP
It is a tidiness measure. For access control use
.BR euclid-cli-eam (1)
grants, which is what decides who may reach a bucket at all.
.SH REVERSIBLE
.B \-\-internal false
puts the bucket back in the listing exactly as it was. Nothing about the
bucket or its objects changes either way; only whether listings mention
it.
.PP
A flag that could only be set is one nobody would dare set.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fINAME\fR
Bucket to change. Required. A bucket name is enough; a full ERN also
works and is what reaches another namespace.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-internal " " \fItrue\fR|\fIfalse\fR
Whether to hide it. Defaults to \fItrue\fR \- naming a bucket without
saying is asking for it to be hidden, which is what the command is for.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The flag was set. The bucket's name and new state are printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing arguments, no session, the bucket does not exist, it belongs to
another account, the caller has no grant for it, or the server rejected
the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
Hide the bucket applications are deployed from:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm set-bucket-internal --bucket apps
.fi
.RE
.PP
Put it back in the listing:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm set-bucket-internal --bucket apps --internal false
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-create-bucket (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-buckets (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1)
